    <section class="cf-section" id="footing-type">
      <h2>Footing Types — Which Do You Need?</h2>
      <table class="cf-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Shape</th><th>Used For</th><th>Typical Size</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Strip / Continuous</strong></td><td>Long rectangle</td><td>Foundation perimeter, load-bearing walls, retaining walls</td><td>12–24 in wide × 8–18 in deep</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Pad / Isolated</strong></td><td>Square or rectangle</td><td>Column bases, deck piers, post bases</td><td>18–36 in square × 12–24 in deep</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Round (Post Hole)</strong></td><td>Cylinder</td><td>Fence posts, deck posts, mailbox, signs</td><td>6–12 in diameter × 18–36 in deep</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Mat / Raft</strong></td><td>Large slab</td><td>Entire building on poor soil</td><td>Engineered — requires structural PE</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section class="cf-section" id="depth-guide">
      <h2>Frost Depth Guide by US Region</h2>
      <p>Footings must be placed below the local frost line — otherwise freeze-thaw cycles will heave the footing, cracking walls and structures above. Always verify with your local building department.</p>
      <table class="cf-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Region / State</th><th>Frost Depth</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Florida, Southern TX, Southern CA</td><td>0 inches</td><td>No frost depth concern</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mid-Atlantic (VA, NC, TN)</td><td>8–12 inches</td><td>Light frost risk</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Pacific Northwest (OR, WA)</td><td>12–18 inches</td><td>Mild winters — varies by elevation</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Ohio Valley, Kansas, Missouri</td><td>18–24 inches</td><td>Moderate frost</td></tr>
          <tr><td>New England, Great Lakes (MI, WI)</td><td>36–48 inches</td><td>Deep frost — verify locally</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Minnesota, North Dakota</td><td>42–60 inches</td><td>Some of deepest frost depths in US</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mountain West (CO, high elevation)</td><td>24–48 inches</td><td>Varies by altitude</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section class="cf-section" id="post-hole">
      <h2>Concrete Post Hole Guide</h2>
      <p>Fence and deck post holes are the most common use of bagged concrete for DIY projects. Here's how much you need for standard configurations:</p>
      <table class="cf-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Post Size</th><th>Hole Dia.</th><th>Depth</th><th>ft³ per hole</th><th>Bags needed</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>4×4 wood / 2" steel</td><td>8 in</td><td>24 in</td><td>0.70 ft³</td><td>2 × 40 lb bags</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4×4 wood (standard)</td><td>10 in</td><td>24 in</td><td>1.09 ft³</td><td>2 × 60 lb bags</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6×6 wood / 4" steel</td><td>12 in</td><td>30 in</td><td>1.96 ft³</td><td>3–4 × 60 lb bags</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mailbox / sign post</td><td>6 in</td><td>18 in</td><td>0.29 ft³</td><td>1 × 40 lb bag</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="cf-info">💡 <strong>Quikrete Fast-Setting Concrete tip:</strong> For fence posts, you can pour the dry mix directly into the hole and add water on top — no mixing required. This method sets in 20–40 minutes, allowing you to continue with the next post. Standard concrete still works fine but requires mixing first.</div>
    </section>

    <section class="cf-section" id="faq">
      <h2>Footing FAQ</h2>
      <div class="cf-faq-item open">
        <div class="cf-faq-q">How deep should a concrete footing be?</div>
        <div class="cf-faq-a">Footings must be below the local frost line to prevent heaving. In the South, 8–12 inches may be sufficient. In the Midwest and Great Lakes, 36–48 inches is standard. In Minnesota and North Dakota, frost depths can reach 60 inches. Always check with your local building department — they will have the code-required frost depth for your specific zip code.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="cf-faq-item">
        <div class="cf-faq-q">How many bags of concrete for a fence post hole?</div>
        <div class="cf-faq-a">For a standard 4×4 fence post with a 10-inch diameter × 24-inch deep hole: you need approximately 1.09 ft³ of concrete. Use <strong>2 × 60 lb bags</strong> (0.45 ft³ each = 0.90 ft³, slightly under) or <strong>2 × 80 lb bags</strong> (1.20 ft³ total) for a comfortable margin. The Post Holes mode in this calculator gives exact quantities for any hole diameter, depth, and number of posts.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="cf-faq-item">
        <div class="cf-faq-q">What is the minimum footing size for a residential structure?</div>
        <div class="cf-faq-a">Per the IRC (International Residential Code), minimum continuous footing width for a 1-story structure is 12 inches wide × 6 inches thick; for a 2-story structure, 15 inches wide × 7 inches thick; for a 3-story structure, 23 inches wide × 8 inches thick. These are minimums — your structural engineer or local code may require larger sizes based on soil bearing capacity. Always get a permit for structural footings.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="cf-faq-item">
        <div class="cf-faq-q">How long does concrete footing take to cure before building on it?</div>
        <div class="cf-faq-a">Wait at least <strong>3–7 days</strong> before placing any load on a footing. Full design strength (100%) is reached at 28 days. For structural footings under walls or columns, most engineers require a minimum 7-day cure before loading. In cold weather (below 50°F), cure times extend significantly — use insulated curing blankets and avoid pouring when temperatures drop below 40°F.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="cf-faq-item">
        <div class="cf-faq-q">What is the difference between a footing and a foundation?</div>
        <div class="cf-faq-a">A <strong>footing</strong> is the widened base that distributes the structural load into the soil. It's typically wider than the wall or column above it to spread load over a larger bearing area. The <strong>foundation</strong> refers to the vertical element — wall, piers, or grade beam — that sits on top of the footing and transfers building loads down to it. Think of the footing as the foot and the foundation as the leg. Both must be designed together based on soil bearing capacity and structural loads.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="cf-faq-item">
        <div class="cf-faq-q">How wide does a concrete footing need to be?</div>
        <div class="cf-faq-a">A widely used rule of thumb is that footing width should be at least <strong>twice the width of the wall</strong> it supports. For a standard 8-inch concrete block wall, the footing should be at least 16 inches wide. For an 8-inch poured concrete wall, a 16-inch wide footing is the IRC minimum for a one-story structure. Wider footings are required on soft or expansive soils where the bearing capacity is lower than average.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="cf-faq-item">
        <div class="cf-faq-q">Can I pour a footing in cold weather?</div>
        <div class="cf-faq-a">Concrete should not be poured when ambient temperatures are below <strong>40°F</strong> without cold-weather precautions. Options include: insulated curing blankets, heated enclosures, hot water in the mix, or accelerating admixtures. Fresh concrete that freezes before achieving at least 500 PSI will lose up to 50% of its final design strength — damage that cannot be reversed. Many contractors refuse to pour footings when a freeze is forecast within 24 hours of the pour.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="cf-faq-item">
        <div class="cf-faq-q">Do I need rebar in a concrete footing?</div>
        <div class="cf-faq-a">Yes, for most structural footings. Horizontal rebar near the bottom of a strip footing adds tensile strength to resist bending from uneven soil pressure and differential settlement. Most building codes require a minimum of <strong>two #4 (½-inch) horizontal bars</strong> in continuous footings, placed 3 inches from the bottom. Vertical dowels are required where the footing connects to a poured concrete wall above. Post hole footings for fences and mailboxes do not require rebar unless specified by local code.</div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cf-section" id="footing-worked-example">
      <h2>Worked Examples: Footing Concrete Calculations</h2>
      <h3>Example 1 — Strip Footing for a Garage</h3>
      <p>A detached 2-car garage (20×24 ft) needs a continuous perimeter footing. The footing is 16 inches wide × 10 inches deep.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Perimeter: (20 + 24) × 2 = <strong>88 linear feet</strong></li>
        <li>Width in feet: 16 / 12 = 1.333 ft</li>
        <li>Depth in feet: 10 / 12 = 0.833 ft</li>
        <li>Volume (net): 88 × 1.333 × 0.833 = <strong>97.8 ft³ = 3.62 yd³</strong></li>
        <li>With 10% waste: <strong>3.98 yd³ — order 4.0 yd³</strong></li>
        <li>Bags alternative: 3.98 yd³ × 45 bags/yd³ = 179 × 80 lb bags</li>
      </ul>
      <p>At $160/yd³, material cost is ~$638. At $10.50/bag, bags would cost ~$1,880 — nearly 3× more. Ready-mix is clearly the right choice here.</p>

      <h3>Example 2 — Pad Footings for a Deck (6 posts)</h3>
      <p>A deck is supported by 6 posts, each on a 24×24-inch pad footing, 12 inches deep.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Volume per pad: 2.0 × 2.0 × 1.0 = 4.0 ft³</li>
        <li>Total for 6 pads: 4.0 × 6 = <strong>24.0 ft³ = 0.89 yd³</strong></li>
        <li>With 10% waste: <strong>0.98 yd³</strong></li>
        <li>80 lb bags: ⌈26.4 / 0.60⌉ = <strong>44 bags</strong></li>
        <li>Bag cost: 44 × $10.50 = <strong>~$462</strong></li>
        <li>Ready-mix: 0.98 yd³ × $160 + $140 short-load fee = <strong>~$297</strong></li>
      </ul>
      <p>Even with the short-load fee, ready-mix is cheaper for this volume — but bags are more convenient if truck access to the deck location is difficult.</p>

      <h3>Example 3 — Fence Post Holes (20 posts)</h3>
      <p>A 100-foot privacy fence uses 20 posts (5-foot spacing), with 10-inch holes, 30 inches deep.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Volume per hole: π × (5/12)² × (30/12) = 3.1416 × 0.1736 × 2.5 = <strong>1.36 ft³</strong></li>
        <li>Total: 1.36 × 20 = <strong>27.2 ft³</strong></li>
        <li>With 10% waste: 29.9 ft³ = <strong>1.11 yd³</strong></li>
        <li>80 lb bags: ⌈29.9 / 0.60⌉ = <strong>50 bags</strong></li>
      </ul>
      <p>At ~$10.50/bag, total bag cost is ~$525. Most DIYers use bags for fence posts because the work is spread over multiple days and Quikrete Fast-Setting Concrete allows you to move immediately to the next post without waiting.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="cf-section" id="soil-bearing">
      <h2>Soil Bearing Capacity and Footing Size</h2>
      <p>The size of your footing is determined not just by what's above it, but by what's below it. Soil bearing capacity — the ability of soil to support a load without excessive settlement — varies significantly by soil type.</p>
      <table class="cf-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Soil Type</th><th>Bearing Capacity (psf)</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Bedrock</td><td>12,000–100,000+ psf</td><td>Best possible bearing — footings can be minimal</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Dense gravel / gravel-sand</td><td>3,000–8,000 psf</td><td>Good bearing — typical suburban soils</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Dense sand</td><td>2,000–4,000 psf</td><td>Good — verify compaction after excavation</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Loose sand / fill</td><td>500–2,000 psf</td><td>Requires wider footings or compaction</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Stiff clay</td><td>1,500–4,000 psf</td><td>Watch for expansion when wet</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Soft clay / peat</td><td>500–1,000 psf</td><td>High risk — may require deep footings or pilings</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>If you are unsure of your soil type, a licensed geotechnical engineer can conduct a soil test for $300–$800 — money well spent before pouring a large structural footing. Most local building departments will specify a minimum assumed bearing capacity (typically 1,500–2,000 psf) for residential permits; if your soil is weaker, you may need to increase footing width or switch to a different foundation system.</p>
    </section>
  </div>

